## Goal
The user wants the navigator eval to be a suite that gets tracked like the other evals. Today it is a separate runner, `evals/navigator/{run.mjs,lib.mjs,run.test.mjs,README.md}`. That runner writes its own `history.jsonl` outside the repo, at `../ambicode-evals-assets/bench/results-nav/`. It runs cases in date order on ONE persistent bench repo, `../ambicode-evals-assets/bench/fe-angular`. That repo is NDA, so its content must never enter the repo. The repo's `.ambicode/navigator/features.md` carries over from run to run. Truth comes from `archive/evals/common/core/cases/<id>/` via `archive/evals/nav/score.mjs`, which is gitignored.

## Step 1: find how tracking works today
Read `evals/README.md`, `evals/scripts/` (cases.mjs, the runner behind `npm run evals` and `npm run evals:bare`, and whatever writes `evals/history.jsonl`, `evals/history.*` and `evals/chains/*`), and the `package.json` scripts. Find out four things:
- what a tracked record looks like;
- how a suite or case is identified;
- how per-case score, cost and time are stored;
- how the plugin and bare arms and the "lift" are computed.

Also check whether the `claude plugin eval` case format (a case dir with prompt.md or case.yaml, `context.scaffold_script` and graders) could run the bench. Each run there starts in an empty workspace. Could a scaffold script copy or link the persistent bench, and write features.md back afterwards? Say yes or no with evidence. If yes, the suite also becomes runnable from the app's eval tool with per-case progress.

