Also check whether the `claude plugin eval` case format (a case dir with prompt.md or case.yaml, `context.scaffold_script` and graders) could run the bench. Each run there starts in an empty workspace. Could a scaffold script copy or link the persistent bench, and write features.md back afterwards? Say yes or no with evidence. If yes, the suite also becomes runnable from the app's eval tool with per-case progress.

## Step 2: implement the smallest integration
Make navigator runs land in the same tracking as the other suites. Pick the mechanism from step 1:
- Option A: the navigator runner writes records in the existing history format into `evals/history.jsonl`, with a suite name such as `navigator`, its case ids, arm (navigator or bare), scores (recall and precision as the case score, or whatever the existing records use for score), cost, duration, and model, plus the existing chain or aggregate output if other suites produce one.
- Option B: plugin-eval cases with a scaffold, as described above.

Prefer the one that reuses the existing code path; don't build a second history format. History records must contain only ids, numbers and repo-relative paths, never prompt or ticket text. Bench and truth paths come from env/args as now. A machine without the NDA assets must fail clearly with a single line, and must not break `npm run evals` or the tests.
- Add an npm script, e.g. `npm run evals:navigator`, beside the existing `evals` scripts.
- Update `evals/README.md` with a short section.

## Verify ($0)
- Unit tests for the record mapping. `node --test` on the changed test files.
- A dry or import mode that converts the existing run-2 artifact into ONE tracked record, so the history shows the real run. The artifact is `../ambicode-evals-assets/bench/results-nav/history.jsonl` plus its run dir: case fe-vs-6086-investigate, navigator arm, cost $0.097, full recall 0.147, full precision 0.556, nav recall 0.263. Show that the record appears wherever other suites' records are read, for example the summary or chain script output, if one exists.
- Grep the tests that assert on the files you touched and run them. Run the existing `evals/scripts` tests. Do NOT run full verify.

## Report (≤20 lines)
- how tracking works: the record format in 1–2 lines;
- whether plugin-eval cases could carry the persistent bench, yes or no and why;
- which option you chose and why, plus the rejected option in one line;
- the files changed;
- the test results, with real output;
- the imported record line;
- the summary output that shows it;
- any open issues.

